                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                        "entry_id": "85149704-0d9e-3dce-add1-4c608c5374f2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To split."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "ambitransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5dcc7210-6e83-4918-bc8b-815d6d08730e",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "Piſcem exdorſuare, […] To ſpleete out, oꝛ part alongeſt the ridge bone iuſt in the midſt."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "14de9976-e18d-4ba9-ad04-fba226d75790",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "Of the ſpleeting in the ſhoulder, or renting the ſhoulder from the breaſt of the Horſe."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e1e3835-6929-4b23-9153-d4233d78342b",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "[I]t offends mee to the ſoule, to heare a robuſtious perwig-pated fellowe tere a paſſion to totters, to very rags, to ſpleet the eares of the groundlings, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "35f46de2-ac6e-4086-b144-5a359ca86934",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "[M]ine owne tongue / Spleet’s what it ſpeakes: […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b6dd9a44-c3b3-4a97-8103-533dd54a3bbb",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "Hurdles, made in form of Gates, either of ſpleeted Timber or of Hazle Rods, they either ſerve for gates in Encloſures or to make Sheep-folds or the like."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f733ce93-5b71-40f4-93cc-d558ee5cf68b",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "And at all times it is highly dangerous, for any not experienced with theſe Seas, to paſs through between the Iſles, tho with ſmall Boats, becauſe of the many blind Rocks lying there, upon which sometimes the Inhabitants themſelves do ſpleet, […] The ſignal defeat and overthrow of the Spaniſh Armado […] was eſpecially cauſed by ſtormy Winds and Tempeſts, some thereby ſinking at Sea, others ſpleeting upon the Coaſts both of England and France and eſpecially upon the North of Scotland, Orkney and Zetland; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "48d14977-6058-4eeb-829d-94f17616a7dc",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "[T]here were the men at ease, holding their sides, laughing like to spleet them; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e5697b51-8d8b-4f21-b515-32bcbf02c4ec",
                                "sense_id": "32ea5f9b-9ebb-3a39-b85d-87c5f61fd0eb",
                                "text": "Da auld axe ta spleet da lamb’s head wi’, Sh[etland] News (Sept. 22, 1900)"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
